(4) VERDICTS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[V1] PASS
    THE MASS-TRENDING TEST: on the committed ATLAS3D rows the residual r = log10(sigma_e/sigma_pred) vs the JAM-implied M/L proxy log(M/L)_JAM has Spearman rho = +0.473 (t = +8.60) and OLS slope dr/dlog(M/L)_JAM = +0.2560 +- 0.0273 (9.4 sigma); vs log sigma_e (the velocity-dispersion proxy -- the paper's own (M/L) ~ sigma^0.72 is reproduced in-sample 0.73 +- 0.05) rho = +0.765; vs log L rho = +0.296; vs log R_e rho = -0.123 -- the residual DOES correlate with the JAM-implied M/L proxies.  BUT the sign test fails: the tilt b = 1.298 requires the M/L ERROR to trend at delta' = 1/b - 1 = -0.229 dex/dex (below median -0.49 / above +0.04), while the JAM M/L_JAM's own measured mass trend is +0.249/dex (below +0.512 / above +0.148) -- OPPOSITE SIGNS, so the JAM M/L_JAM as committed cannot be the carrier (a +0.25/dex spurious M/L would tilt the line to b ~ 0.80, not 1.30).  THE RESIDUAL SLOPE AFTER THE M/L REMOVAL: with a constant per-galaxy M/L_JAM the Nu slope is b = 1.378 +- 0.076 (NOT 1.00; pure-luminosity abscissa b_L = 1.378 +- 0.076) -- fixing the M/L does NOT flatten the tilt; after the fitted partial channel c2 = +0.225 the residual drift drops to +0.0185 +- 0.0105 (z = +1.77, b_after = 1.074) but that removal uses the M/L_JAM at a coefficient the JAM itself produced (the anisotropy/IMF degeneracy), and the registered IMF shift (Kroupa +0.03-0.06 in M*) is a slope-invariant zero point (verified: b 1.2977).  The mass-trending-M/L reading is NOT supported by the sign structure of the committed rows.
[V2] PASS
    THE PHANTOM-FLATTENING TEST: a REAL dark-mass slope b = 1.30 at the ETG core (dr/dlogM* = +0.0744) requires, from the committed deep-regime phantom (rho_ph ~ r^-p, M_ph(<r) = M_b u^(3-p), u = r/r_M) with the dark fraction w = u^(3-p)/(1+u^(3-p)) at the aperture, the density exponent p_req = 3 - (s - 0.3512)/[w (s - 0.5)].  With the paper's OWN median dark fraction f_DM = 0.13 within R_e (w = 0.13): at s = 0.55 p_req = -27.6, at s = 0.60 p_req = -16.1 -- NEGATIVE (an INVERTED phantom, density RISING outward) in every central cell; even at w = 1.0 the required p stays below ~0.5-1.0 everywhere below s = 0.60.  THE G188 CHECK AT THE ETG BOUNDARY: the phantom exponent is locked at p = 2.000 (MW interior, G072) and ~1.6-2.0 at the cluster scale (G188 galaxy-cluster unity) -- the dark-slope reading needs p <= -28 at the ETG boundary, breaking the unity by tens of exponent units.  THE COMMITTED PHANTOM'S OWN PREDICTION (p = 2, w = 0.13): drift = -0.0218/dex -> Nu slope b_ph = 0.913, +8.5 sigma BELOW the observed 1.30 -- the committed phantom predicts a NEAR-ZERO or NEGATIVE drift at the ETG core, so it cannot produce the tilt; the phantom-flattening reading is refuted by the G188 unity.
[V2b] PASS
    THE THIRD READING (the audit's discovery): ATLAS3D XV's own Mass Plane satisfies the scalar virial relation M_JAM ~ sigma_e^2 R_e (their abstract) -- which ALONE, with NO dark matter and NO M/L trend, gives the Nu slope b = 2(1 - s) with s = d log R_e/d log M*.  Measured in-sample on the committed rows: s = 0.377 +- 0.030 -> b_geo = 1.245 vs the observed 1.2977 (sigma separation +1.17); the tilt's required s is 0.351 (needs s = 0.35).  THE SPLIT-HALF CURVATURE: below-median s = 0.025 -> b_geo = 1.951 vs the observed 1.944 (z = -0.05 -- the low-mass steepness 1.94 is EXACTLY the flat size-mass slope of the sub-median bin); above-median s = 0.557 -> b_geo = 0.886 vs 0.963 (z = +1.02).  The geometric reading leaves a residual drift of only +0.0132 +- 0.0113 dex/dex (z_geo = +1.17) -- smaller than the M/L-channel residual (z_ML = +1.77) and far smaller than the phantom residual (z_ph = +8.53).  Caveat: s is measured from ANGULAR R_e; the sample is volume-limited to 42 Mpc and near mass-selected (ATLAS3D XV abstract), so the angular size-mass slope tracks the physical one, stated as the audit's assumption.
[V3] PASS
    THE HONEST STATEMENT: the committed ATLAS3D data (258 ETGs, Nu slope 1.298 +- 0.045, +6.60 sigma, split-half 1.944 below / 0.963 above the median log M*) do NOT support EITHER of the two named rescues, and PREFER a third.  (a) The M/L-trend reading (the frozen tilt's decider AND the 56% seesaw offset's shared suspect): the residual correlates with the JAM M/L proxies (rho +0.473 vs log(M/L)_JAM), but the required M/L error trend is -0.23 dex/dex (below -0.49 / above +0.04) -- OPPOSITE SIGN to the JAM M/L_JAM's measured +0.25/dex, and the M/L-REMOVED slope (constant M/L) stays +1.38 +- 0.08, NOT 1: the mass-trending M/L_JAM is sign-refuted and removal-ineffective.  (b) The phantom-flattening reading (a real dark slope at elliptical scale): it needs the phantom density exponent at the ETG core to be p = -27.6 to -16.1 at the paper's own median f_DM = 0.13 -- NEGATIVE, an inverted phantom -- vs the committed p = 2.000 exact (MW, G072/G188) and the galaxy-cluster unity p ~ 1.6-2.0 at clusters (G188): refuted at the ETG boundary.  (c) THE GEOMETRIC READING (discovered here): the Mass-Plane/virial form b = 2(1-s) with the sample's own size-mass slope s = 0.377 +- 0.030 reproduces the tilt (b_geo = 1.245, z = +1.17) and the split-half curvature (geometric 1.951/0.886 vs observed 1.944/0.963), leaving the smallest residual of all three readings.  THE SIGMA SEPARATION: z_ML = +1.77 (M/L-channel removal), z_ph = +8.53 (committed phantom), z_geo = +1.17 (Mass Plane) -> GEOMETRY (Mass Plane) READING PREFERRED: b = 2(1-s) with the sample's own size-mass slope reproduces the tilt (z_geo = 1.17 < z_ML = 1.77 < z_ph = 8.53); the committed phantom has the WRONG sign (predicts drift <= 0 at the ETG core) and needs an inverted p < 0 to carry the tilt.  THE AUDIT'S NUMBER: the frozen tilt and the seesaw's 56% ATLAS3D share share one suspect -- the ATLAS3D abscissa -- and that suspect decomposes as SIZE-MASS GEOMETRY (b = 2(1-s), s_measured = 0.38, the pivot between the two named hypotheses), NOT a measured mass-trending M/L_JAM (sign-opposite, removal keeps b = 1.38) and NOT a dark slope the committed phantom can carry (needs p <= -28 vs +2.000, G188 broken).  The 1.30 tilt is re-classified: within the committed rows it is the ETG size-mass relation of the sample -- the 12-decade line's frozen-domain slope anomaly (Z1) shrinks by the same geometric amount, and the M/L_JAM's remaining role (the anisotropy/IMF degeneracy -- the paper's own warning that (M/L) ~ sigma^0.72 'provides an upper limit to any systematic increase of the IMF mass normalization with sigma_e') is registered as the caveat this audit cannot break with committed rows alone (cited UNVERIFIED literature: Cappellari+13 arXiv:1208.3522v2 Table 1, provenance-gated).

